package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q1 {
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter no: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        for (int i = 1; i <=n ; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=n; j++) {
                System.out.print("* ");

            }
            System.out.println();

        }

    }
}

OUTPUT:
n = 3
* * *
* * *
* * *


Approach
Take an integer n as input from the user.
Use an outer loop to print n rows.
For each row, use an inner loop to print n stars (*).
After printing one complete row, move to the next line.
Repeat the process until all n rows are printed.


Logic
The outer loop controls the number of rows.
The inner loop controls the number of columns.
Since both loops run from 1 to n, a total of n × n stars are printed, forming a square pattern.


Time Complexity
Outer loop runs n times.
Inner loop runs n times for each iteration of the outer loop.

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity

Space Complexity: O(1)
